 ## Overview
-Distributed Volume Raycasting with Bricks is a parallel rendering technique that subdivides a volumetric dataset into convex subvolumes (bricks), assigning each to a separate node in a cluster for independent GPU raycasting [S1]. It differs from single-GPU methods by distributing the computational load across multiple machines and using software-based compositing to handle the final image assembly, thereby avoiding hardware blending limitations and `glReadPixels` performance bottlenecks [S1].
+Distributed Volume Raycasting with Bricks is a parallel rendering technique that subdivides a volumetric dataset into convex subvolumes (bricks), assigning each to a separate node in a cluster for independent GPU raycasting [S1]. It differs from single-GPU methods like [GPU Ray Casting with Fast Intersection](7032-gpu-ray-casting-with-fast-intersection.md) and [GPU-based Ray Casting](7131-gpu-based-ray-casting.md) by distributing the computational load across multiple machines and using software-based compositing to handle the final image assembly, thereby avoiding hardware blending limitations and `glReadPixels` performance bottlenecks [S1].
 
